\subsection{Theoretical Motivation: Why Entropy Leads Diversity}
\label{sec:theory}

The empirical lead-lag relationship between token entropy and lexical diversity (\S\ref{sec:regime}) admits a simple theoretical motivation grounded in the distinct mathematical properties of continuous and discrete summary statistics.
We present three arguments that, taken together, predict the observed temporal ordering and its dependence on contamination strength.
These arguments provide intuition rather than formal proof; the dose-response experiments in \S\ref{sec:regime} test the resulting predictions empirically.

\paragraph{Continuous versus discrete response.}
Token entropy $H(p_t^{(g)}) = -\sum_v p_v \log p_v$ is a continuous functional of the token distribution $p_t^{(g)}$ at generation $g$: any infinitesimal shift in $p_t^{(g)}$ produces a proportional change in $H$.
Distinct-$n$ diversity $D_k^{(g)}$, by contrast, is a counting statistic.
An $n$-gram contributes to $D_k^{(g)}$ only when its probability exceeds an effective sampling threshold $\tau_n \approx 1/N$, where $N$ is the number of generated tokens.
Below $\tau_n$, the $n$-gram is absent from finite samples regardless of its true probability.
$D_k^{(g)}$ therefore responds to distributional change in discrete steps: it remains constant while probabilities drift within the interval above $\tau_n$, then drops abruptly when a probability crosses the threshold.
Because $H$ tracks every distributional perturbation while $D_k^{(g)}$ remains silent until a threshold crossing occurs, entropy is sensitive to distributional change strictly before diversity registers it.

\paragraph{Predicted detectability regimes.}
The continuous-versus-discrete asymmetry determines not just the ordering of responses but the \emph{detectability} of their temporal offset.
We model Granger-type detectability as the product of two competing factors:
\begin{equation}
    \text{Detectability}(\alpha) \;\propto\; S(\alpha) \times R(\alpha),
    \label{eq:detectability}
\end{equation}
where $S(\alpha)$ is the \emph{signal strength} (the magnitude of distributional shift induced by contamination) and $R(\alpha)$ is the \emph{temporal resolution} (the generational gap between the entropy response and the diversity response).

$S(\alpha)$ increases monotonically with $\alpha$: more synthetic data induces larger distributional shifts.
$R(\alpha)$, by contrast, is non-monotonic.
At low $\alpha$, entropy and diversity respond in different generations ($R$ large); at a critical intermediate $\alpha$, \emph{relaxation time matching} occurs---the perturbation strength aligns the entropy and diversity relaxation times so that both metrics shift within the same generation, driving $R \to 0$.
At high $\alpha$, the perturbation is severe enough that entropy collapses within generation~0$\to$1 (the continuous-functional advantage saturates immediately), while diversity still requires generation~1$\to$2 to fully manifest the loss---tail $n$-grams near $\tau_n$ persist temporarily before crossing the threshold---and the one-generation lead recovers ($R$ large again).

The competition between $S$ and $R$ predicts four regimes:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Blind} ($\alpha \leq 0.45$).
$S$ falls below the detection floor; $S \times R \approx 0$ regardless of $R$.
\item \textbf{Onset} ($\alpha \approx 0.50$).
$S$ crosses the detection threshold while $R$ remains high (entropy leads diversity by a full generation); $S \times R$ is sufficient for detection.
Between the blind and onset regimes, the framework predicts a \emph{canary-only window} in which entropy shifts detectably but no $n$-grams have yet crossed $\tau_n$; we lack the $\alpha$ resolution to confirm this (our grid jumps from $0.25$ to $0.50$) and leave verification to future work with finer spacing in $[0.10, 0.45]$.
\item \textbf{Synchronous dip} ($\alpha \approx 0.60$).
$S$ continues increasing, but $R$ drops sharply: relaxation time matching at this $\alpha$ compresses the entropy--diversity lag to near zero, producing a local minimum in $S \times R$.
\item \textbf{Detection} ($\alpha \geq 0.75$).
$S$ grows large enough to dominate the product despite reduced $R$; the continuous-functional advantage reasserts across generations, and $S \times R$ recovers.
\end{enumerate}
These four regimes align with the empirical dose-response (\S\ref{sec:regime}): blind at $\alpha{\leq}0.45$ (PermG 0/8), onset at $\alpha{=}0.50$ (6/8, dual-seed), synchronous dip at $\alpha{=}0.60$ (0--3/8, dual-seed), and detection at $\alpha{\geq}0.75$ (5--6/8 per seed, three seeds; 6/8 per seed at $\alpha{=}1.0$, four seeds).
The non-monotonic profile is a prediction of the $S \times R$ framework, not an anomaly requiring post-hoc explanation; indeed, non-monotonic dose-response curves are well-documented across disciplines.
The four-regime profile mirrors the hormetic dose-response in toxicology, where intermediate doses produce qualitatively different effects from both low and high doses~\cite{calabrese2003hormesis}.
The temporal resolution collapse at intermediate $\alpha$ ($R \to 0$) corresponds to the known failure of Granger-type tests under contemporaneous coupling~\cite{deshpande2010instantaneous}: when entropy and diversity shift within the same generation (lag${=}0$), the causal test loses statistical power by construction, not by methodological deficiency.
The detection dip is also consistent with critical speeding-up phenomena in ecological regime shifts, where early-warning indicators exhibit non-monotonic behavior near phase boundaries rather than the monotonic rise predicted by classical critical slowing down~\cite{titus2020speeding}.
We emphasize that the $S \times R$ model provides qualitative intuition, not quantitative derivation; the regime boundaries depend on model capacity, dataset properties, and sample size $N$.

\paragraph{Detection threshold vs.\ accumulated contamination.}
The detection phase transition at $\alpha_c{=}0.476$ (\S\ref{sec:regime}) reveals that Granger detectability depends on the \emph{per-generation} mixing ratio $\alpha$, not on cumulative contamination.
After $G{=}10$ generations, cumulative synthetic fraction reaches $C(\alpha, G) = 1 - (1{-}\alpha)^G$: at $\alpha{=}0.45$, $C = 99.75\%$; at $\alpha{=}0.50$, $C = 99.90\%$---a difference of 0.15 percentage points---yet PermG detection jumps from 0/8 to 6/8.
The explanation lies in the per-generation signal.
PermG detects temporal lead-lag from the time series of inter-generation entropy shifts $\{\Delta h^{(g)}\}_{g=1}^G$, where $\Delta h^{(g)} \propto \alpha \cdot d_{\mathrm{TV}}(p_g^{\mathrm{model}}, p^{\mathrm{real}})$ captures the per-episode perturbation scaled by the distributional gap at generation~$g$.
The critical threshold $\alpha_c$ corresponds to the per-generation signal-to-noise ratio crossing the detection floor: below $\alpha_c$, each episode's signal is too faint for the Granger test to distinguish from noise at $T{=}11$, regardless of how many episodes accumulate.
This overturns the implicit assumption in data poisoning research that detection difficulty scales with total contamination; our results show that two training runs with nearly identical cumulative exposure ($99.75\%$ vs.\ $99.90\%$) produce categorically different detection outcomes.

\paragraph{Statistical power interpretation.}
The $S \times R$ framework admits a formal statistical grounding: the detection phase transition at $\alpha_c$ corresponds to a \emph{power threshold} rather than a physical phase transition.
Under a permutation test framework, detection power scales as $\mathrm{Power}(\alpha, T) = \Phi\bigl(S(\alpha) \cdot R(\alpha) \cdot \sqrt{T} \,/\, \sigma_{\mathrm{noise}}\bigr)$, where $\Phi$ is the standard normal CDF.
When $S(\alpha) \cdot R(\alpha) \cdot \sqrt{T}$ first exceeds the detection threshold, power rises sharply from near-zero to near-one, producing the sigmoid-like onset observed empirically.
This formalization generates three testable predictions for longer generation chains ($T{=}20$, currently running):
(i)~$\alpha_c$ should \emph{left-shift} (increased $T$ raises power at all $\alpha$, lowering the detection threshold);
(ii)~the transition should \emph{smooth} ($\sqrt{T}$ scaling broadens the power curve, making onset less step-like); and
(iii)~different methods should exhibit \emph{different} $\alpha_c$ values (each method has its own power curve, so PermG and MMCT weighted consensus may cross the detection threshold at different $\alpha$).
Prediction~(iii) remains a theoretical consequence awaiting empirical validation from $T{=}20$ replication; at $T{=}10$, sub-threshold PermG $p$-values at $\alpha{=}0.45$ (FDR $p{=}0.059$--$0.091$; \S\ref{sec:regime}) are consistent with proximity to the power boundary but do not yet demonstrate method-dependent $\alpha_c$.

\paragraph{Connection to effective rank and detection layers.}
The link between entropy and diversity can be made more concrete through effective rank~\cite{roy2007effective}: $\mathrm{erank}(P) = \exp(H(P / \|P\|_1))$ measures the number of dimensions carrying substantial probability mass.
A decline in $H$ contracts $\mathrm{erank}(P)$, pushing tail tokens below $\tau_n$ and eliminating the $n$-grams they participate in.
This yields a mechanistic chain---$H$ decreases $\to$ $\mathrm{erank}$ contracts $\to$ tail probabilities cross $\tau_n$ $\to$ $D_k^{(g)}$ drops---in which entropy responds first.
This chain mediates the temporal resolution factor $R(\alpha)$: at intermediate $\alpha$ (${\approx}0.60$), relaxation time matching compresses the erank-mediated cascade into a single generation ($R \to 0$); at higher $\alpha$, the severity of entropy collapse outpaces the cascade, restoring the generational offset ($R$ large; \S\ref{sec:regime}).

Effective rank is a \emph{distributional dimensionality} indicator---it detects contraction of the distribution's effective degrees of freedom---and is conceptually distinct from both distributional instability (variance inflation) and classical critical slowing down (CSD) indicators (rising autocorrelation) that characterize dynamical stability near tipping points~\cite{scheffer2009early,dakos2012methods}.
These layers, together with the causal coupling layer captured by Granger-type tests, form four complementary detection channels ordered by onset threshold (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).
The distinction matters because the layers can diverge: \citet{truong2025entropy} show that entropy collapse can be a first-order phase transition under convex feedback dynamics, in which CSD indicators remain bounded before the critical point and provide no advance warning.
In such regimes, distributional dimensionality may register contraction before dynamical indicators respond---or, as our experiments show (\S\ref{sec:case_study}), may fail entirely in an architecture-dependent manner.

\paragraph{Phase transition theory and the onset boundary.}
The onset at $\alpha{\approx}0.50$---quantified empirically as $\alpha_c{=}0.476$ via sigmoid regression (\S\ref{sec:regime})---may reflect a critical mixing threshold analogous to the scaling law of \citet{gu2024cmr} for continual pre-training, and resonates with the finding of \citet{gu2025datamixing} that data mixing itself can induce phase transitions in knowledge acquisition.
The non-monotonic dip at $\alpha{=}0.60$ (\S\ref{sec:regime}) is consistent with the two universality classes of \citet{truong2025entropy}: if the transition is first-order (Class~1, irreversible, exponent $\nu{=}1$), the abrupt simultaneous shift in canary and downstream metrics suppresses the temporal lead that Granger tests exploit, while smoother second-order dynamics (Class~2, reversible, $\nu{=}1/2$) at neighboring $\alpha$ values preserve it.
These connections are speculative and require finer $\alpha$ resolution and direct estimation of feedback nonlinearity to validate.

